<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palazzo Madama, Turin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palazzo Madama is a palace on Piazza Castello in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> that grew from a Roman gate and a medieval castle of the Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> branch of the dynasty. It takes its name from two royal widows, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christine-of-france">Christine of France</a> and Marie Jeanne Baptiste of Savoy-Nemours, who lived there as regents in the seventeenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a> gave it a baroque façade in 1718–1721. It later housed the Subalpine Senate and is now Turin's civic museum of ancient art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzo-madama-turin.jpg" alt="Palazzo Madama, Turin" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lurens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palazzo_Madama,_Torino_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
